Scope of work
The role of the Fundraising Officer is to source and onboard major donors to advance the Nalafem’s fundraising and awareness raising efforts, and to create and oversee the implementation of the organization’s fundraising strategy. The position is responsible for identifying and building relationships with top prospective foundations, corporations, and individual donors as well as submitting grant proposals.
Duties and responsibilities
Develop and implement Nalafem fundraising strategy in line with Nalafem’s key flagship projects and strategy through careful research and analysis;
Develop and submit grant proposals ongoing basis
Identify best practices and lessons learned directly linked to fundraising activities and contribute to early monitoring and evaluation efforts;
Develop accurate benchmarks to guide in the projections.
Join all donor meetings
Develop list of top foundation and corporate prospects at from local to global levels ;
Onboard major donors, and plan, implement, manage, and coordinate the stewardship activities of Nalafem’s major donors;
Ensure appropriate and timely follow-up of donors following the receipt of pledges and funds through a professional stewardship AI powered infrastructure;
Ensure Nalafem finds sponsors for special events throughout the year such as the fellowship and Nalafem Summit.
Develop high quality financial and narrative reports, ensuring consistency, accuracy, clarity and timely submission in compliance with Nalafem as well as donor contractual requirements;
Ensure compliance with Nalafem and donor requirements and compliance with the gifts acceptance policy, on the uses of funds, including budget monitoring, amendment requests and timely flagging of problems;
Maintain and regularly update Nalafem's donor and partner database, ensuring accurate records of contact details, giving history, and engagement activity;
Draft and send quarterly in collaboration with Director, update emails to partners and donors, highlighting key achievements, impact, and funding needs;
Qualifications and skills
At least 5 years of professional experience in the nonprofit development field, and a proven record of securing major contributions from foundations, corporations, and additional sources; experience working for nonprofit(s) of similar scope and size is a plus.
An acute understanding of the data infrastructure required for systematic resource mobilization especially AI powered.
Interpersonal and communications skills and capacity to build excellent relationships with key stakeholders, particularly with high-level individuals in a multinational environment.
Experience researching and securing funds from organizations; access to high net worth databases and subscriptions to capacity research tools is a plus.
Comfort with and/or experience of operating in a lean, start-up environment.
Strong communication in written and speaking English and French is a strong bonus.
Strong organizational and time management skills with exceptional attention to detail.

Responsibilities
In close coordination with the Regional Office and IOM Global Office in Washington, D.C., relevant Units at Headquarters (HQ), the IOM offices in the region, Government counterparts and other relevant public/private sector partner organizations in the geographical area of the response, contribute to drafting/reviewing protection assessments and identify risk mitigation strategies focusing on, among others, operational settings and accessibility of services, including shelter, legal frameworks, protection risks for specific vulnerable groups, key protection actors on the ground and available referral pathways, safe environments for return in Countries of Origin (COO), etc.
In coordination with the MPX Regional Thematic Specialist (RTS) and IOM Global Office in Washington, D.C., provide technical guidance and support to national and international protection staff in the EHSA Region to ensure high-quality outcomes for migrants, while advising on operational coordination and ensuring adherence to IOM policies.
Ensure appropriate coordination in line with IOM and interagency standards. Participate in relevant coordination mechanisms or task forces at the local level and/or at the regional level, as relevant. Contribute to the implementation of inter-agency coordination standards and the development of context-specific guidance as relevant.
Provide remote and in-person thematic support to Country Offices in the context of the setup of the response and provision of assistance, ensuring a coherent approach across all interventions. Be available for short-notice international deployments outside the duty and home station, when required, in order to support urgent protection responses.
Represent RO EHSA in technical meetings and forums, including with donors, partners, and stakeholders, as required.
Identify emerging operational priorities and contribute to the design and development of project proposals and activities based on solid results frameworks, in response to emerging trends and priorities, as well as donor interests and requirements.
Support the Senior Regional Migrant Assistance Specialist in case management coordination, including providing technical input to the review of complex Assisted Voluntary Return (AVR) cases and internal approval processes, in line with IOM protection policies and standards.
Contribute to developing and delivering specialized training on AVR, case management, and protection interventions. Contribute to the exchange of lessons learned, best practices, and tools at the regional and intra-regional level.
Perform such other duties as may be assigned.
Qualifications
Required Qualifications and Experience
Education
Master’s degree in Social Sciences, International Relations/Affairs, Research, Law, or a related field from an accredited academic institution with five years of relevant professional experience; or,
University degree in the above fields with seven years of relevant professional experience.
Accredited Universities are those listed in the UNESCO World Higher Education Database.
Experience
Experience working with migrants, civil society, and governments;
Experience working in multicultural and multidisciplinary teams;
Proven experience supporting protection programming, as well as mainstreaming protection across operations, preferably across multiple countries;
Proven experience providing protection and assistance to migrants in vulnerable situations and communicating with individuals who have complex needs;
Experience working in return and reintegration programs;
Experience in capacity building, training delivery, and the development of operational tools and guidance is preferred; and,
Experience with IOM or other UN agencies, and specialized protection programs, is preferred.
Skills
Strong knowledge of IOM and inter-agency protection standards and operational procedures and guidelines;
Solid coordination skills;
Ability to research and adapt to different operational and cultural contexts;
Strong writing, editing, presentation, and training facilitation skills;
Excellent computer skills, including proficiency in MS Word, Excel, PowerPoint, email/internet software, and relevant statistical analysis packages;
Knowledge of UN cooperation frameworks and bilateral donor programming requirements;
Ability to gather, consolidate, and attribute information from multiple sources;
High degree of judgment and initiative, with the ability to work independently within assigned responsibilities;
Strong interpersonal and teamwork skills; and,
Proven ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with people of diverse cultural and national backgrounds.
